---
// Individual contribution and leadership, built from the Figma frames "Leadership" (1680),
// "Leadership / 1280", "Leadership / 1024" and "Leadership / Mobile 320".
// The hero quote is real text on a CSS gradient. Its colours stay the same in dark mode,
// because white on accent_default only works with the light-mode accent.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Link from '../components/Link.astro';
import Kpis from '../components/case-study/Kpis.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';
import '../styles/case-study.css';

const results = [
  { figure: '10 designers', description: 'led across Italy and India.' },
  { figure: 'Multiple products', description: 'in the BigFix family held to one architecture.' },
  { figure: '3 designers / 2 developers', description: 'working under my design authority on Mandala.' },
  { figure: '700+ people', description: 'trained through the accessibility programme I built.' },
  { figure: '18 colour themes', description: 'all WCAG 2.2 AA, with 0 contrast defects.' },
  { figure: '3 languages', description: 'worked in natively: Italian, English and Danish.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Individual contribution and leadership"
  description="How I lead ten designers across Italy and India: owning the BigFix UI, a handoff framework that makes accessibility part of every screen, and a team built on happiness and trust."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1 class="hero__title">Individual contribution and leadership</h1>
      <figure class="hero-quote">
        <span class="hero-quote__mark" aria-hidden="true">“</span>
        <blockquote class="hero-quote__text">
          <p>The courage of leadership is giving others the chance to succeed even though you bear the responsibility for getting things done.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption class="hero-quote__author">Simon Sinek</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <Kpis label="In numbers" items={results} />

    <div class="section text">
      <p>I lead by staying close to the work.</p>
      <p>
        My team is ten designers across Italy and India, all working on the UI of BigFix. I set the direction, define the standards and hold the final sign-off on how
        every screen looks and behaves. I mentor every designer on the team, and I stay hands-on in the strategy, the systems and the delivery that sit underneath the
        screens.
      </p>
      <p>My influence comes from craft. I can explain why a decision is right, show how to make it, and be trusted to get it right when it matters.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="own">
      <h2 id="own">What I own</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          I own the UI of BigFix, in a role that did not exist until it was created for me. That means the UI architecture, the accessibility standards and the
          governance of the design system, across multiple products in the BigFix family. It also means full responsibility for design readiness: nothing goes into
          development from my team until it is clear, complete and accessible, so developers get their answers before they have to ask.
        </p>
        <p>I steer the effort alongside stakeholders and leadership across the United States, India and Italy, in several meetings every week.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="team">
      <h2 id="team">How the team works</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          One of the things I am proudest of is having built a team people are happy to be part of, where everyone collaborates. We all work hard, but if someone
          needs help, they can barely finish the sentence before someone offers.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have interviewed candidates and reviewed portfolios and I run team meetings every week so that everyone has the context, the trust and the room they need
          to do their best work.
        </p>
        <p>
          The designers are also strong on accessibility, and thoughtful about it. It is embedded not only in what they deliver but in how they think: they ask the
          accessibility question at the start of a design, not at the end. When security restrictions ruled out off-the-shelf Figma plugins, they built their own to
          speed up handoff. Nobody asked them to. It is a good example, and the clearest sign that the system works because people understand it, not because I
          enforce it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="quality">
      <h2 id="quality">Quality that scales with the team</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          My role has shifted from hands-on design to direction, review and final sign-off, and the team takes on more of the review every month. That way the quality
          of BigFix is carried by many people instead of resting on one. I keep the final sign-off and the standard it is held to. The judgement behind it is
          increasingly shared, which is what lets the work move quickly across multiple products.
        </p>
        <p>
          The handoff framework is how that works in practice. It sets out how every screen behaves from large desktop to mobile, and every accessibility decision a
          designer controls: page regions, heading hierarchy, tab order, labels on icon-only buttons, alt text, spacing tokens and correct component usage. It gives
          developers a clear answer, gives QA a standard to test against, and draws a clean line of responsibility. Design guarantees accessibility on its side, and
          development and QA own it from there.
        </p>
        <p>The team has made the framework their own, and they extend it on their own initiative.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="beyond">
      <h2 id="beyond">Leading beyond my team</h2>
      <h3>Mandala</h3>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          I hold design authority on the system and make the final call on every decision in it. Three designers work with me on the Figma side and two developers
          sign the code off. None of us works on Mandala full time, because everyone has a demanding job alongside it, so the structure has to work without anyone
          being available all the time. Gates, written rules and a decision log carry that load. A person reviews the design and a person reviews the code, and that
          rule has never had an exception.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h3>Accessibility</h3>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          I designed and delivered a structured accessibility programme for HCLSoftware: 12 sessions, 18 hours, 25 teams and more than 700 people from design,
          engineering, QA and product. Sessions are timed against each team's own roadmap, so people apply what they learn within weeks. Several senior leaders asked
          for the recordings to be kept as a library for future hires. Nobody had to attend, and people kept coming back.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="countries">
      <h2 id="countries">Working across countries</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          I lead distributed design teams across multiple countries and time zones, and I work natively in Italian, English and Danish. In practice that means writing
          things down clearly, making decisions visible to people in every time zone, and building shared standards so that no one needs to be in the room to do good
          work.
        </p>
        <p>
          It also means writing in one language while speaking in another, and occasionally speaking to someone in the wrong language, because I often no longer know
          which one I am in.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="ai">
      <h2 id="ai">Leading with AI</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Knowledge and experience lead, and AI augments that work. In Mandala I use Claude for work that does not need a human hand at every step, such as keeping
          Figma and Storybook in sync and checking contrast at a scale no manual process could match. Every gate, every decision and every judgement call stays human.
        </p>
        <p>
          I also mentor the three designers working with me on Mandala as they take their first steps in the AI panorama. The same principle applies to leading
          people: I use tools to remove friction and keep the judgement with the people who have earned it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="section text text-body_bold">
      <p>
        The measure of this work is not any single screen. It is that the team understands the system well enough to take it further, and that the standard holds
        wherever the work goes.
      </p>
      <p>And it is the team itself.</p>
      <p>
        Happiness and trust are the foundation of everything described here, and I do not think they are a soft extra. People who feel safe asking for help, who trust
        each other's judgement and who enjoy the work together produce better designs, catch more problems and keep learning. That is what I am most proud to have
        built.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="section section--padded read-next">
      <section class="read-next__item" aria-labelledby="next-mandala">
        <h2 id="next-mandala">Mandala case study</h2>
        <div><Link href={url('/mandala/')}>Read the case study</Link></div>
      </section>
      <section class="read-next__item" aria-labelledby="next-about">
        <h2 id="next-about">About me</h2>
        <div><Link href={url('/about/')}>Learn more about me</Link></div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Figma "Hero quote": white on a light spot of accent at the top, accent_default (light mode) at the edges.
     The lightest point gives white 3.3:1, which the large text needs; phones get a smaller spot so the
     18px attribution stays above 4.5:1. */
  .hero-quote {
    --hero-quote-light: #7c88d7;
    --hero-quote-edge: #5363ca;

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--space_8);
    row-gap: var(--space_8);
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space_32) var(--space_24) var(--space_32) var(--space_16);
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse 70% 90% at 46% 0%, var(--hero-quote-light), var(--hero-quote-edge));
    color: #ffffff;
  }

  .hero-quote__mark {
    grid-row: span 2;
    font-family: var(--font-instrument-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 7rem;
    line-height: 5rem;
  }

  .hero-quote__text {
    margin: 0;
    padding-block-start: var(--space_16);
    font-family: var(--font-instrument-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    line-height: 2rem;
  }

  .hero-quote__author {
    grid-column: 2;
    justify-self: end;
    font-family: var(--font-instrument-sans);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.75rem;
  }

  @media (width >= 47.5em) {
    .hero-quote {
      column-gap: var(--space_16);
      padding: var(--space_64) var(--space_64) var(--space_64) var(--space_32);
      background: radial-gradient(ellipse 75% 125% at 46% 0%, var(--hero-quote-light), var(--hero-quote-edge));
    }

    .hero-quote__mark {
      font-size: 11.625rem;
      line-height: 8.3125rem;
    }

    .hero-quote__text {
      padding-block-start: var(--space_24);
      font-size: 2.5rem;
      line-height: 3.75rem;
    }

    .hero-quote__author {
      font-size: 1.75rem;
      line-height: 2.625rem;
    }
  }

  /* 1680 layout */
  @container main (width >= 77rem) {
    .hero-quote__mark {
      font-size: 14rem;
      line-height: 10rem;
    }

    .hero-quote__text {
      font-size: 3rem;
      line-height: 4.5rem;
    }

    .hero-quote__author {
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 3rem;
    }
  }

  /* Figma "Section: Read next": two headed links 32 apart */
  .read-next {
    gap: var(--space_32);
  }

  .read-next__item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_16);
  }
</style>
